  ('3ac065f4-b259-52e1-af1f-b36fd6fe77e5', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-narrative-writing'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The writer wants to make sentence 2 more vivid while keeping the same point of view and purpose. Which revision does this best?$q$, $q$[{"text":"So when she hands me the keys this summer, I will probably assume that she has made a mistake.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"shifts-tense"},{"text":"So when she dropped the keys into my palm the summer I turned seventeen, I stared at them as if they belonged to someone else.","is_correct":true},{"text":"So when she handed her grandchild the keys that summer, the grandchild assumed she had made a mistake.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"shifts-point-of-view"},{"text":"So when she gave me the keys, I felt something about it, and it seemed strange in some way.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"vague-language"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$So when she dropped the keys into my palm the summer I turned seventeen, I stared at them as if they belonged to someone else.$q$, $q$The correct choice keeps the first person and past tense while adding a precise action and image that show the narrator's disbelief. The first choice switches to third person, breaking the narrator's point of view.$q$, 'draft', '14f45f66-490f-575b-9b5b-685ec761fe27')
